WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:03.400
♪♪

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.934
-I thought last week
was really good.

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:07.267
[ Laughter ]

00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:09.634
It was the kind
of "Corrections" episode

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:12.801
that I think best represents
what we're trying to do here.

00:00:12.801 --> 00:00:16.467
And when people say to me,
"Where should I start?"

00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:19.601
I want to be able
to say episode 82,

00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:21.467
but I know that I'm going
to follow up saying,

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.868
"But there's a lot of stuff
in 81 that sets it up.

00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:27.501
And if you're going to watch 81,
you just got to watch --"

00:00:27.501 --> 00:00:30.667
And at some point you're like,
"Start from the beginning."

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:32.601
And it's a day.

00:00:32.601 --> 00:00:33.868
[ Laughter ]

00:00:33.868 --> 00:00:37.234
You're asking people
for a human day.

00:00:37.234 --> 00:00:39.734
So, you know, look,
I'm glad you're here

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:41.634
because I don't think
we're picking up new people.

00:00:41.634 --> 00:00:45.434
[ Laughter ]

00:00:45.434 --> 00:00:48.834
You know what I mean?
I think it's us.

00:00:48.834 --> 00:00:52.968
And it's just --
it's going to --

00:00:52.968 --> 00:00:56.934
It's just going to
slowly degrade over time

00:00:56.934 --> 00:01:01.901
just as people, you know, age
out, rest in peace situation.

00:01:01.901 --> 00:01:05.167
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:07.467
The prime minister
of Australia's name

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.801
is pronounced
Anthony "Alban-ase"--

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:13.767
"Alban-easy."
Albanese, rhymes with "easy."

00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:16.667
I just wish there was
a simple way to remember that.

00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:19.300
[ Light laughter ]

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:20.934
Well, you know, we were
talking about him

00:01:20.934 --> 00:01:22.534
because we were joking
that the state dinner,

00:01:22.534 --> 00:01:25.200
he had a state dinner
at the White House

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.734
and the White House
served beetroot.

00:01:27.734 --> 00:01:30.601
And we were saying
that's insulting.

00:01:30.601 --> 00:01:33.767
You know, to have
a world leader over

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:38.434
and give him something as
uninteresting as beetroot.

00:01:38.434 --> 00:01:40.133
And then a bunch of you
wrote beetroot

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:42.000
is very common
in Australian cuisine,

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.868
so it was appropriate to serve.
And hey, I get it.

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:47.701
If they ever threw a dinner
for me at the White House,

00:01:47.701 --> 00:01:50.534
I would definitely want them
to make stuff I have at home.

00:01:50.534 --> 00:01:54.400
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.767
"We can do anything you want.
We're the White House."

00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:01.567
"You got a pantry?
I'd love to quietly eat

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:03.567
a handful of almonds
in the dark."

00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:08.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:13.100
I hope this doesn't come off as
critical of Australian cuisine.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.000
I love --

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.701
I'm going out
for Australian tonight.

00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:19.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:22.300
Can't get enough of it.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.367
When I go on Seamless
or GrubHub,

00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:28.167
I just always go
to that cuisine tab,

00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:32.367
see what my local Australian

00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:33.567
has for the night.

00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:37.200
I have so much respect
for Australian chefs

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.667
because pretty much
every creature that lives there,

00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:45.167
if you cook it
and serve it to a person,

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:47.634
that act in any other country
in the world

00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:49.934
is considered attempted murder.

00:02:49.934 --> 00:02:51.534
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.067 --> 00:02:57.167
When an Australian waiter
comes to your table,

00:02:57.167 --> 00:03:00.501
the first thing they say is
"Before I tell you the specials,

00:03:00.501 --> 00:03:01.968
is anyone here a cop?"

00:03:01.968 --> 00:03:07.267
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:11.400
I did my Australian waiter

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.467
in my regular voice
because the feedback

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:17.300
was I have
a terrible Australian accent.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.100
It was bad last week.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:22.767
It was bad last week because
I did not do my vocal warm-ups

00:03:22.767 --> 00:03:24.734
that I need to do
for my Australian accent,

00:03:24.734 --> 00:03:26.400
and I did not
do my vocal warm-ups

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.133
because I'm told it's very
unsettling to watch me do them.

00:03:30.133 --> 00:03:36.567
Like, [Grunting]

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:38.901
[ Laughter ]

00:03:38.901 --> 00:03:41.100
It's like that for 10 minutes.

00:03:47.434 --> 00:03:49.534
[ Laughter ]

00:03:49.534 --> 00:03:51.734
Oh, I mentioned in
"A Closer Look" yesterday

00:03:51.734 --> 00:03:53.968
that I went to see
"PAW Patrol 2" this weekend,

00:03:53.968 --> 00:04:00.200
and I intimated that I did not
enjoy it, but that is my fault

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:04.200
that I didn't enjoy it because
I never saw "PAW Patrol 1."

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.734
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.734 --> 00:04:09.167
So I was having a real hard time
just following it.

00:04:09.167 --> 00:04:11.400
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:15.434
That movie is very ploddy.

00:04:15.434 --> 00:04:17.400
That's ploddy with two D's.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.434
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.434 --> 00:04:25.767
I just didn't quite understand
why these dogs could talk.

00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:30.100
Why they were being funded by
the military industrial complex.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.667
I don't mind when animals
talk in movies.

00:04:32.667 --> 00:04:35.934
I just don't like
when they can talk to people.

00:04:38.501 --> 00:04:42.167
There have to be rules.

00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:44.968
We had a picture of Donald Trump
shoplifting

00:04:44.968 --> 00:04:48.434
an "Us Weekly" in yesterday's
"Closer Look" as well.

00:04:48.434 --> 00:04:52.467
And a bunch of you complained
because he was putting a

00:04:52.467 --> 00:04:55.434
"Us Weekly" --
he was stealing a Kit Kat.

00:04:55.434 --> 00:04:57.234
And a bunch of you
said his hands

00:04:57.234 --> 00:04:59.601
in the graphic were too big.

00:04:59.601 --> 00:05:02.934
And so I pulled it up.
I don't -- I don't know,

00:05:02.934 --> 00:05:05.601
I sometimes feel like
you're -- you think like I like

00:05:05.601 --> 00:05:07.968
when you call out graphics

00:05:07.968 --> 00:05:10.400
and their errors,
but I don't know.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:11.434
I think it's fine.

00:05:11.434 --> 00:05:15.501
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.934 --> 00:05:21.100
Oh, we had the "leave him alone"
guy on the show this week,

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.701
and someone said
he tapped his phone

00:05:24.701 --> 00:05:29.734
when it was clearly
a slide-to-answer situation.

00:05:29.734 --> 00:05:32.734
We apologize. And next Monday
to make it up to you,

00:05:32.734 --> 00:05:34.133
we're going to do
the whole thing again.

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:37.968
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.968 --> 00:05:41.334
Start to finish.

00:05:41.334 --> 00:05:43.667
Hey, we made a joke about Pepsi.

00:05:43.667 --> 00:05:45.501
We had a Pepsi fan
who took umbrage

00:05:45.501 --> 00:05:47.901
and said our joke fell flat.
Just like Pepsi.

00:05:47.901 --> 00:05:51.300
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.801 --> 00:05:54.734
I feel like I did a good job

00:05:54.734 --> 00:05:58.100
saying "pleading" this week
instead of "pled."

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.367
But I did let
a "pled" slip in once,

00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:02.300
and for that I apologize.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.100
We've spent a lot of time
already talking about

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.434
the correct past tense for
"plea."

00:06:06.434 --> 00:06:07.868
I don't want to
get into it again.

00:06:07.868 --> 00:06:10.000
Just know that
if I make a mistake,

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.434
we will catch it internally.

00:06:11.434 --> 00:06:13.133
We're trying to correct it.
We're trying to get it right.

00:06:13.133 --> 00:06:18.033
If you hear it again, just --
just let it go.

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:20.334
Please.

00:06:20.334 --> 00:06:21.934
I am "pledding" with you.

00:06:21.934 --> 00:06:23.501
[ Laughter ]

00:06:26.834 --> 00:06:28.667
[ Laughter ]

00:06:29.968 --> 00:06:32.934
Someone wrote in a criticism,
"Making fun of elders,

00:06:32.934 --> 00:06:35.801
is this what passes
as comedy now?"

00:06:35.801 --> 00:06:36.767
Elders?

00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:38.434
[ Laughter ]

00:06:38.434 --> 00:06:39.901
How old are you?

00:06:39.901 --> 00:06:43.000
[ Laughter ]

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.133
Also, someone wrote
in "Corrections" --

00:06:45.133 --> 00:06:48.667
this is a comment
in "Corrections" --

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:52.100
"So, the writers
are still on strike?"

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:55.033
Rude.

00:06:55.033 --> 00:06:57.267
We joked you could lure Trump
into a pan --

00:06:57.267 --> 00:07:00.267
into a van, into a pan.
Ooh.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:03.734
Maybe they didn't notice.

00:07:03.734 --> 00:07:07.434
[ Laughter ]

00:07:07.434 --> 00:07:10.000
We joked you could lure Trump
into a van with a puppy.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.400
Many of you correctly
pointed out

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.400
that Trump hates dogs so much,
that wouldn't work

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:18.033
unless you said it like,
"Hey, kid.

00:07:18.033 --> 00:07:20.968
You want to see us [bleep]
with this poodle?"

00:07:20.968 --> 00:07:24.801
[ as Trump ]
I would like to see that.

00:07:24.801 --> 00:07:27.000
I'm a little kid.
I'd love to see that.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.534
[ Normal voice ]
Ken Chesebro.

00:07:31.534 --> 00:07:36.834
He said "I'm guilty" this week.

00:07:36.834 --> 00:07:40.701
He aid it in a courtroom.

00:07:40.701 --> 00:07:43.901
And because of Ken
Chesebro's name,

00:07:43.901 --> 00:07:46.234
we said --

00:07:46.234 --> 00:07:50.834
We made a run of jokes
about cheese-based Hasbro toys.

00:07:50.834 --> 00:07:56.033
One of them was Barbrie.
Barbie is Mattel.

00:07:56.033 --> 00:07:58.901
I got her confused with that
other doll, Oppenheimer.

00:07:58.901 --> 00:08:02.534
[ Laughter ]

00:08:02.534 --> 00:08:05.234
My Little Pony is Hasbro,
which was the other one.

00:08:05.234 --> 00:08:08.300
I made a joke,
My Little Provolone.

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:09.467
Killed.

00:08:09.467 --> 00:08:12.100
[ Laughter ]

00:08:13.667 --> 00:08:15.267
One of you wrote, "That joke
doesn't work

00:08:15.267 --> 00:08:18.300
because the E
in 'provolone' is silent."

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.968
You know what else is silent?
The audience

00:08:19.968 --> 00:08:22.000
the day I let you
write my punch lines.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.534
[ Laughter ]

00:08:31.634 --> 00:08:35.534
I also want to stress
in case you like that,

00:08:35.534 --> 00:08:40.667
this isn't going to become
a sort of cheese-pun place.

00:08:40.667 --> 00:08:46.067
That was that. That's over,
because I could tell

00:08:46.067 --> 00:08:49.968
that was bouncing around
the Internet like, "Hey, whoa!

00:08:49.968 --> 00:08:51.234
I thought he just did
political jokes,

00:08:51.234 --> 00:08:54.734
but there are some good --
good cheese puns."

00:08:57.667 --> 00:08:59.467
We said Trump's lumpy coat
looks like a guy

00:08:59.467 --> 00:09:01.701
trying to sneak a bunch of kids
into an R-rated movie.

00:09:01.701 --> 00:09:03.601
A bunch of you pointed out he
wouldn't need to sneak them in

00:09:03.601 --> 00:09:05.934
because they just have to be
with a guardian,

00:09:05.934 --> 00:09:08.400
so he could just
bring them in, but that assumes

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:11.367
that Trump
wants to pay for their tickets.

00:09:13.934 --> 00:09:16.234
I said the plural of Pegasus
was Pegasi.

00:09:16.234 --> 00:09:19.167
Pegasus is a proper name,
and even if it wasn't,

00:09:19.167 --> 00:09:21.167
the plural would be Pegasuses.

00:09:22.734 --> 00:09:25.667
Pegasi would be if it was Roman,
but it's Greek.

00:09:27.834 --> 00:09:29.501
Pegasus was created
when it sprang

00:09:29.501 --> 00:09:32.634
from the neck of a Medusa

00:09:32.634 --> 00:09:36.000
after she was beheaded
by Perseus.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.734
That was unique
to that Medusa.

00:09:38.734 --> 00:09:41.501
It doesn't happen
to every Medusi.

00:09:41.501 --> 00:09:47.434
[ Laughter ]

00:09:47.434 --> 00:09:50.734
Have you tried the new Medusa
cheese, Shoemaker?

00:09:50.734 --> 00:09:52.534
[ Laughter ]

00:09:52.534 --> 00:09:53.767
Gorgonzola.

00:09:53.767 --> 00:10:00.167
[ Laughter ]

00:10:05.934 --> 00:10:08.200
[ Laughter ]

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:11.367
Speaking of Shoemaker,

00:10:11.367 --> 00:10:12.701
I went to his daughter's
wedding this Saturday.

00:10:12.701 --> 00:10:14.267
Oh, my God, what a night.

00:10:14.267 --> 00:10:16.834
Beautiful, beautiful wedding.

00:10:16.834 --> 00:10:18.901
Beautiful bride.

00:10:18.901 --> 00:10:20.734
Here's the thing though.

00:10:20.734 --> 00:10:22.601
I've worked with Shoemaker
for over 20 years.

00:10:22.601 --> 00:10:27.133
Everyone in my life knows

00:10:27.133 --> 00:10:32.267
how close we are, knows
how important he is to me.

00:10:32.267 --> 00:10:35.534
There's not a person I know that
wouldn't be able to tell you

00:10:35.534 --> 00:10:37.801
everything about Mike Shoemaker.

00:10:37.801 --> 00:10:40.734
I go to this wedding.

00:10:40.734 --> 00:10:45.167
I'm meeting his neighbors,
cousins, his family, friends.

00:10:45.167 --> 00:10:46.701
Every person there walks up
to me and says,

00:10:46.701 --> 00:10:48.934
"Seth Meyers?
What are you doing here?"

00:10:48.934 --> 00:10:54.801
[ Laughter ]

00:10:54.801 --> 00:10:56.267
To a person.

00:10:56.267 --> 00:11:00.334
[ Laughter ]

00:11:00.334 --> 00:11:07.033
And I'm like,
"Oh, Mike's the producer

00:11:07.033 --> 00:11:10.067
of my television show."

00:11:10.067 --> 00:11:13.133
And they're like,
"Mikey the Shoe?"

00:11:13.133 --> 00:11:15.200
[ Laughter ]

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:19.734
Like, not only do these people
not know he's a TV producer,

00:11:19.734 --> 00:11:22.367
like, they can't believe
he has a TV.

00:11:22.367 --> 00:11:24.534
[ Laughter ]

00:11:24.534 --> 00:11:27.868
They're all like, "A producer?

00:11:27.868 --> 00:11:31.901
We thought he cleaned
swimming pools."

00:11:31.901 --> 00:11:33.767
And then I catch his eye
across the room,

00:11:33.767 --> 00:11:35.701
and he's giving me one of these,
like...

00:11:35.701 --> 00:11:37.834
[ Laughter ]

00:11:37.834 --> 00:11:39.334
I walk over, I go, "What?"

00:11:39.334 --> 00:11:43.133
He goes,
"Stop blowing up my spot."

00:11:43.133 --> 00:11:44.601
I go, "What are you
talking about?

00:11:44.601 --> 00:11:46.834
Why do you not want these
people to know what you do?"

00:11:46.834 --> 00:11:49.033
He goes, "It's none
of their business."

00:11:49.033 --> 00:11:51.501
And I go, "What do you
tell them you are?"

00:11:51.501 --> 00:11:53.634
And then he says to me,
"Dead broke."

00:11:53.634 --> 00:11:57.100
[ Laughter ]

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:58.400
"You think they'd lend me money

00:11:58.400 --> 00:11:59.934
if they know what I do
for a living?"

00:11:59.934 --> 00:12:02.434
[ Laughter ]

00:12:02.434 --> 00:12:04.534
And I'm like,
"But you have money."

00:12:04.534 --> 00:12:06.267
And he goes,
"You can never have enough."

00:12:06.267 --> 00:12:08.834
And that's not -- At a wedding.

00:12:08.834 --> 00:12:14.567
[ Laughter ]

00:12:14.567 --> 00:12:18.234
Main takeaway though,
it was amazing.

00:12:18.234 --> 00:12:23.133
Katherine, his daughter,
a beautiful bride.

00:12:23.133 --> 00:12:24.367
Married a wonderful guy.

00:12:24.367 --> 00:12:27.033
Very funny comedian
named Mike Antonucci.

00:12:27.033 --> 00:12:28.567
And I remember my wedding day

00:12:28.567 --> 00:12:33.834
and how much I wish
I could live it over again.

00:12:33.834 --> 00:12:40.067
And I walked over to the groom
and I put a hand on his shoulder

00:12:40.067 --> 00:12:42.801
because I wanted to tell him,
"Don't let this day fly by."

00:12:42.801 --> 00:12:43.868
And I put my hand
on his shoulder.

00:12:43.868 --> 00:12:46.767
He slaps it away
and he turns around.

00:12:46.767 --> 00:12:49.334
He goes, "Oh, Mikey the Duch,
don't like

00:12:49.334 --> 00:12:50.734
when you touch the merchandise."

00:12:50.734 --> 00:12:51.968
I'm like, "Oh [bleep]"

00:12:51.968 --> 00:12:54.701
[ Laughter ]

00:12:54.701 --> 00:12:57.434
[Bleep] Hey, man.

00:13:01.067 --> 00:13:02.501
A pumpkin is a pumpkin.

00:13:02.501 --> 00:13:05.734
A jack-o'-lantern is a carved
pumpkin with a light in it,

00:13:05.734 --> 00:13:07.868
and a jackal lantern
is a pumpkin

00:13:07.868 --> 00:13:10.567
that criticizes trick-or-treater
costumes

00:13:10.567 --> 00:13:11.667
when they come
to the front door.

00:13:11.667 --> 00:13:13.000
They're like, "Eh, actually,

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:14.834
Harry Potter
wouldn't wear New Balance."

00:13:14.834 --> 00:13:18.767
[ Laughter ]

00:13:21.267 --> 00:13:23.534
Somebody wrote this.
We've been talking about venom

00:13:23.534 --> 00:13:25.868
versus poison.

00:13:25.868 --> 00:13:28.167
Somebody wrote,
"Venom usually isn't poisonous,

00:13:28.167 --> 00:13:30.534
so ingesting venom
wouldn't kill you

00:13:30.534 --> 00:13:34.567
unless you had some sort of open
wound in your digestive tract

00:13:34.567 --> 00:13:36.234
so the venom
got in your blood."

00:13:36.234 --> 00:13:38.000
Well, I thought
that was assumed,

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:41.300
but if you want me
to say that every time.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:44.000
[ Laughter ]

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.834
I also talked about
the differential --

00:13:45.834 --> 00:13:50.067
I tried to differentiate
between ingested and injected,

00:13:50.067 --> 00:13:55.234
and I put the emphasis
on the syllable they share.

00:13:55.234 --> 00:13:58.167
I was like,
"IN-gested means this."

00:13:58.167 --> 00:14:01.000
And then I went
"IN-jected means this."

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:02.367
And that's not
the way you do it.

00:14:02.367 --> 00:14:04.934
You stress the difference.

00:14:04.934 --> 00:14:07.634
It'd be like saying,

00:14:07.634 --> 00:14:09.968
"Oh, JEFF Daniels.

00:14:09.968 --> 00:14:12.534
I thought you meant
JEFF Bridges."

00:14:12.534 --> 00:14:18.300
[ Laughter ]

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:21.000
I said "doth" is how people
talk at ren fairs.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.434
It's doth[duth].

00:14:24.434 --> 00:14:25.868
So the correct usage

00:14:25.868 --> 00:14:28.968
would be "Doth
the candlemaker taketh Visa?"

00:14:28.968 --> 00:14:31.334
[ Laughter ]

00:14:35.501 --> 00:14:37.701
Alright.

00:14:37.701 --> 00:14:39.167
This really --
I couldn't believe

00:14:39.167 --> 00:14:41.000
how many of you did this.

00:14:44.067 --> 00:14:46.534
Talked about "Silence
of the Lambs" last week,

00:14:46.534 --> 00:14:48.267
called Hannibal Lecter
the villain.

00:14:48.267 --> 00:14:55.601
Way, way too many of you said
Buffalo Bill is the villain.

00:14:55.601 --> 00:14:58.334
Hannibal Lecter is the antihero.

00:14:58.334 --> 00:15:01.434
If you're not his lawyer,
shut the [bleep] up about that.

00:15:01.434 --> 00:15:05.033
[ Laughter ]

00:15:05.033 --> 00:15:09.367
Somebody wrote he only bit
one person in that movie.

00:15:09.367 --> 00:15:13.234
Oh, well, in that case,
I guess he can babysit my kids.

00:15:13.234 --> 00:15:15.567
[ Laughter ]

00:15:19.434 --> 00:15:23.133
And look, I'm not defending
Buffalo Bill.

00:15:23.133 --> 00:15:26.801
The man is unwell.

00:15:26.801 --> 00:15:31.567
He's so unwell,
he has an actual well.

00:15:31.567 --> 00:15:34.501
[ Laughter ]

00:15:34.501 --> 00:15:37.567
That's a red flag.

00:15:37.567 --> 00:15:38.767
If you're ever
at someone's house

00:15:38.767 --> 00:15:42.334
and they have an inside well,

00:15:42.334 --> 00:15:43.667
get out.

00:15:45.701 --> 00:15:46.934
I also --

00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:51.934
I talked about Hannibal Lecter's
security guards,

00:15:51.934 --> 00:15:53.467
and someone said,
"Hey, it sounds like --

00:15:53.467 --> 00:15:55.734
his prison guards sound like
your security guys,

00:15:55.734 --> 00:15:56.734
John and Jim."

00:15:56.734 --> 00:15:58.334
That would be my [bleep] dream.

00:15:58.334 --> 00:16:01.200
[ Laughter ]

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:05.000
If John and Jim were Hannibal
Lecter's security guards,

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:08.968
it would --
I would run to work each Monday

00:16:08.968 --> 00:16:11.434
to hear the stories
from those two guys

00:16:11.434 --> 00:16:14.801
about being the prison guards
for Hannibal Lecter.

00:16:14.801 --> 00:16:16.567
I would run to work.
John would be,

00:16:16.567 --> 00:16:19.901
"You're not going to believe
what happened this weekend, sir.

00:16:19.901 --> 00:16:21.601
Hannibal Lecter tricks us."

00:16:21.601 --> 00:16:23.367
[ Laughter ]

00:16:23.367 --> 00:16:28.400
"Gets out of his cuffs,
bites Jim."

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:30.334
I'm like, "Oh, my God,
is Jim okay?"

00:16:30.334 --> 00:16:31.968
"Jim's fine.

00:16:31.968 --> 00:16:34.267
Hannibal Lecter's teeth [bleep]
shattered."

00:16:34.267 --> 00:16:36.567
[ Laughter ]

00:16:36.567 --> 00:16:40.634
"And he bites -- I guess
he bites a lot of people,

00:16:40.634 --> 00:16:42.868
but he never bit anybody
like Jim before."

00:16:42.868 --> 00:16:44.601
"Yeah. You don't --
You don't want to bite me."

00:16:44.601 --> 00:16:47.200
[ Laughter ]

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:49.868
"You don't want to bite me."

00:16:49.868 --> 00:16:52.334
"Can I tell you something, Seth?

00:16:52.334 --> 00:16:55.434
His drawings. This guy Lecter,
his drawings are good."

00:16:55.434 --> 00:16:57.601
[ Laughter ]

00:16:57.601 --> 00:16:59.734
"His drawings.
I mean, Agent Starling,

00:16:59.734 --> 00:17:02.767
she's been here one, two times.

00:17:02.767 --> 00:17:04.334
Looks like a picture of her."

00:17:04.334 --> 00:17:07.367
[ Laughter ]

00:17:07.367 --> 00:17:09.234
"What does he do, Jim?
Is it charcoal?"

00:17:09.234 --> 00:17:12.334
"Graphite.
It's a graphite pencil."

00:17:12.334 --> 00:17:22.167
[ Laughter ]

00:17:22.167 --> 00:17:24.200
I said a gavel banged
on a podium.

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:28.868
I corrected that to a gavel
bangs on a lectern.

00:17:28.868 --> 00:17:32.267
And then I was told the gavel
actually makes contact,

00:17:32.267 --> 00:17:37.334
with what is called
a sound block or a gavel plinth.

00:17:37.334 --> 00:17:42.467
Maybe and I mean maybe
I'll say sound block,

00:17:42.467 --> 00:17:46.634
but I'm never saying "gavel
plinth" in front of this crew.

00:17:46.634 --> 00:17:48.601
[ Laughter ]

00:17:48.601 --> 00:17:51.601
If I said plinth on this show,

00:17:51.601 --> 00:17:54.067
Kenny Coyle would pants me
in the hallway.

00:17:54.067 --> 00:17:55.968
[ Laughter ]

00:17:55.968 --> 00:17:57.934
Plinth.

00:17:57.934 --> 00:17:59.467
With this lot.

00:17:59.467 --> 00:18:01.400
[ Laughter ]

00:18:04.467 --> 00:18:09.267
I got pigeons and gulls
mixed up last week.

00:18:09.267 --> 00:18:12.334
I know gulls are the ones
that eat your food on the beach.

00:18:12.334 --> 00:18:17.133
Best version I've ever seen
of that -- two summers ago,

00:18:17.133 --> 00:18:20.634
my son Axel has a bagel.
He is running down the beach.

00:18:20.634 --> 00:18:22.701
He is holding his bagel
in the air like this.

00:18:22.701 --> 00:18:26.501
He is in such -- He's so happy
that he has a bagel

00:18:26.501 --> 00:18:30.534
and he's running towards us.
We have our towel set up.

00:18:30.534 --> 00:18:35.300
Biggest smile on his face,
and a gull

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:40.834
swoops down, takes the bagel
out of his hand, and flies away.

00:18:40.834 --> 00:18:43.534
It looked like it was
something in a [bleep] circus.

00:18:46.234 --> 00:18:48.033
People gasped.
A lot of people saw it.

00:18:48.033 --> 00:18:50.200
People gasped.
They would have applauded

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:52.801
at what the gull had done,
swooping down

00:18:52.801 --> 00:18:55.767
and grabbing a bagel
out of a running child's hand.

00:18:55.767 --> 00:19:00.267
The only reason they didn't
applaud is Axel was sobbing,

00:19:00.267 --> 00:19:01.801
and I had to go over
and be like,

00:19:01.801 --> 00:19:03.133
"I know you're upset.

00:19:03.133 --> 00:19:04.968
An amazing thing just happened."

00:19:04.968 --> 00:19:12.133
[ Laughter ]

00:19:12.133 --> 00:19:13.534
"You're sad about your bagel,
but a great --

00:19:13.534 --> 00:19:15.200
a thing
I will never forget happened."

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:16.501
[ Laughter ]

00:19:19.968 --> 00:19:22.934
We also talked about whether
these beach birds

00:19:22.934 --> 00:19:25.367
are seagulls or herring gulls.

00:19:25.367 --> 00:19:29.701
So I described this one
to an ornithologist.

00:19:29.701 --> 00:19:33.000
The one that did that
to Axel is a ba-gull[bagel].

00:19:34.434 --> 00:19:38.667
[ Laughter ]

00:19:43.534 --> 00:19:44.767
I'll wait.

00:19:44.767 --> 00:19:46.534
[ Laughter ]

00:19:50.367 --> 00:19:54.701
The chair declares
this "Correction" in recess

00:19:54.701 --> 00:19:57.567
subject to the call
of the chair.

00:20:11.167 --> 00:20:17.634
[ Laughter ]

00:20:17.634 --> 00:20:18.734
Oh.

00:20:19.934 --> 00:20:23.167
I almost forgot.

00:20:23.167 --> 00:20:29.000
We discussed whether Stevie
Nicks wore scarves or shawls.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:32.300
We decided she wore shawls.

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:35.133
And she tied scarves
around her mike stand.

00:20:35.133 --> 00:20:38.868
The reason she doesn't
wear scarves is interesting.

00:20:38.868 --> 00:20:40.300
I guess she has a condition
where her

00:20:40.300 --> 00:20:44.534
cervical spine lacks strength.

00:20:44.534 --> 00:20:46.801
And so it's not healthy
to wrap anything around it.

00:20:46.801 --> 00:20:52.167
This whole area just sort of
lacks a structural integrity.

00:20:52.167 --> 00:20:55.801
To put it in layman terms,
Stevie's neck's weak.

00:20:55.801 --> 00:20:58.133
[ Laughter ]

